[[:db/retract 17592186220509 :answer-field/choices 17592186220512] [:db/retract 17592186220509 :answer-field/choices 17592186220513] [:db/retract 17592186220509 :answer-field/choices 17592186220514] [:db/retract 17592186220509 :answer-field/choices 17592186220510] [:db/retract 17592186220509 :answer-field/choices 17592186220511] {:db/id 17592186221414 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-cb1b21bd-521d-5db5-99c4-385125d3ec35"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186221188 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t)-2y(t)+1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4x(t)+y(t)-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. The equilibrium point of the system is $[1, \\, 2]^T.$ What is the system obtained by shifting the equilibrium to the origin?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n![](images/cd/cd022d759a5ce5a8521687d42eaf38053339212301a6b3559ad4bee935f69317.svg)\nwhere ![](images/f5/f51930ce883e8e2f4865d846b38c475e68875565593ad58044125e81f0443718.svg)\n\nTo shift the system's equilibrium from $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} \n= \\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{x}^\\ast,\n$$\n\nwhere ![](images/53/53cec1a04bca84fba279c48cf5a531674040c07e2a1dac12748107b91809ff72.svg)\n\nIn our case, we have\n![](images/05/05e9d31a8413096e60f8d30b8206052375dc7c9df5895568a33b475fca22fc72.svg)\n\nSubstituting $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf{x}'$ into the given system, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}^{′} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}](u+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}])+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=3u(t)-2v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=4u(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186221415 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a1db4f53-42d8-55c9-8ba8-b7dde90ef735" "question-13f56d3d-b69f-5f92-a641-c6c370a1ed21"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186221189 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)+x(t)+y(t)+2 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=5x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+3y(t)+10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. An equilibrium point of the system is $[0, \\, -2]^T.$ What system is obtained by shifting this equilibrium to the origin?" :question/worked-solution "To shift the system's equilibrium from $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} \n= \\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{x}^\\ast,\n$$\n\nwhere ![](images/33/33beedce1869f8f3a894434acc7c0a89a94062470e7dacd8a5e14a2d61ef36b5.svg) and ![](images/ac/ac4b467f8441d1473393728f7e9a05fd36b106044e73c151780b963db77bdbfa.svg)\n\nIn our case, we have\n![](images/7b/7b764933668ae61643bf324c8a1275df1f5c2ca778153103e687f2ce77719c5d.svg)\n\nIn coordinate form, this can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nx(t) = u(t), \\qquad\ny(t) = v(t) - 2, \\qquad\nx'(t) = u'(t), \\qquad\nv'(t) = y'(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now substitute these into the given system.\n\n- Substituting into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{2}(t)+x(t)+y(t)+2 \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & ={u}^{2}(t)+u(t)+(v(t)-2)+2 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)+u(t)+v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =5x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+3y(t)+10 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) & =5u(t)-(v(t)-2)^{2}+3(v(t)-2)+10 \\\\  & =5u(t)-({v}^{2}(t)-4v(t)+4)+3v(t)-6+10 \\\\  & =5u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+7v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=5u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+7v(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186221190 :question/problem "A linear system of ODEs has an equilibrium at $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast.$ One of the nullclines of the system has the equation $y=3x-1$ in the corresponding phase space. The system is then shifted using $\\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{x}^\\ast.$ Which of the following is **guaranteed** to be a nullcline of the shifted system?\n\nI. $y=3x+3$\n\nII. $y=-x$\n\nIII. $y=3x$\n\nIV. $y=-3x$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186221977 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t)+3 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-x(t)+2y(t)-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. The equilibrium point of the system is $[-1,\\,1]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting the equilibrium to the origin?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTo shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $u=[\\begin{aligned}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⇒\\,x=u+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x$ and ${x}^{′}$ into the given system, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}^{′} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}](u+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=3u(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=-u(t)+2v(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186220509 :answer-field/correct "answer-95f16937-cd0b-5a79-8fa5-bf41e531d725" :answer-field/choices ["answer-95f16937-cd0b-5a79-8fa5-bf41e531d725" "answer-bf509ea2-55f9-56fd-8f16-318399695d29" "answer-85ac10bf-7f54-5f23-9b48-972bdcaa5d76" "answer-1b548975-82b4-57b7-b2e8-0a87a8cc8fdf" "answer-67ce320b-106e-566c-80ef-6487f5a73923"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-95f16937-cd0b-5a79-8fa5-bf41e531d725" :answer/id #uuid "95f16937-cd0b-5a79-8fa5-bf41e531d725" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=3u(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=-u(t)+2v(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bf509ea2-55f9-56fd-8f16-318399695d29" :answer/id #uuid "bf509ea2-55f9-56fd-8f16-318399695d29" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=3u(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=2v(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-85ac10bf-7f54-5f23-9b48-972bdcaa5d76" :answer/id #uuid "85ac10bf-7f54-5f23-9b48-972bdcaa5d76" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=3u(t)+2 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=-u(t)+2v(t)-2\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1b548975-82b4-57b7-b2e8-0a87a8cc8fdf" :answer/id #uuid "1b548975-82b4-57b7-b2e8-0a87a8cc8fdf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=3u(t)+3 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=-u(t)+2v(t)-3\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-67ce320b-106e-566c-80ef-6487f5a73923" :answer/id #uuid "67ce320b-106e-566c-80ef-6487f5a73923" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=3u(t)-1 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=-u(t)+2v(t)+1\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-cb1b21bd-521d-5db5-99c4-385125d3ec35" :question/id #uuid "cb1b21bd-521d-5db5-99c4-385125d3ec35" :question/math-academy-id "q-335196" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. The equilibrium point of the system is $[1,\\,1]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting the equilibrium to the origin?" :question/worked-solution "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⇒\\,x=u+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x$ and ${x}^{′}$ into the given system, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}^{′} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}](u+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])+[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]u.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-819f1de9-2d6b-59f9-9ce0-5429b3d56920"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-819f1de9-2d6b-59f9-9ce0-5429b3d56920" :answer-field/id #uuid "819f1de9-2d6b-59f9-9ce0-5429b3d56920" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c95246ee-d4d2-538d-b832-e359e3deb8b8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0f38395a-e0c9-5a4c-9a38-9a34b59867d5" "answer-24616f82-f759-536a-a547-f8851f776bde" "answer-c95246ee-d4d2-538d-b832-e359e3deb8b8" "answer-ff527e63-d538-51a6-b3c5-fcaa3897bb3a" "answer-7e04fe5c-6c47-5e1c-9d6c-94fd18017163"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0f38395a-e0c9-5a4c-9a38-9a34b59867d5" :answer/id #uuid "0f38395a-e0c9-5a4c-9a38-9a34b59867d5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{u}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{aligned}]u" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-24616f82-f759-536a-a547-f8851f776bde" :answer/id #uuid "24616f82-f759-536a-a547-f8851f776bde" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{u}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]u" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c95246ee-d4d2-538d-b832-e359e3deb8b8" :answer/id #uuid "c95246ee-d4d2-538d-b832-e359e3deb8b8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{u}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]u" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ff527e63-d538-51a6-b3c5-fcaa3897bb3a" :answer/id #uuid "ff527e63-d538-51a6-b3c5-fcaa3897bb3a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{u}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7e04fe5c-6c47-5e1c-9d6c-94fd18017163" :answer/id #uuid "7e04fe5c-6c47-5e1c-9d6c-94fd18017163" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{u}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a1db4f53-42d8-55c9-8ba8-b7dde90ef735" :question/id #uuid "a1db4f53-42d8-55c9-8ba8-b7dde90ef735" :question/math-academy-id "q-335386" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+6y(t)-12 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)+2x(t)+y(t)-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. An equilibrium point of the system is $[1,\\,3]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting this equilibrium to the origin?" :question/worked-solution "To shift the system’s equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}],\\,u=[\\begin{aligned}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\,⇒\\,x=u+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nIn coordinate form, this can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nx(t)=u(t)+1,\\,y(t)=v(t)+3,\\,{x}^{′}(t)={u}^{′}(t),\\,{v}^{′}(t)={y}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now substitute these into the given system.\n\n- Substituting into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =3x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+6y(t)-12 \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =3(u(t)+1)-(v(t)+3)^{2}+6(v(t)+3)-12 \\\\  & =3u(t)+3-({v}^{2}(t)+6v(t)+9)+6v(t)+18-12 \\\\  & =3u(t)-{v}^{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{2}(t)+2x(t)+y(t)-6 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) & =(u(t)+1)^{2}+2(u(t)+1)+(v(t)+3)-6 \\\\  & =({u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+1)+2u(t)+2+v(t)+3-6 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)+4u(t)+v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=3u(t)-{v}^{2}(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+4u(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-11f2b70f-bcb2-5554-a4f5-52982774bf66"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-11f2b70f-bcb2-5554-a4f5-52982774bf66" :answer-field/id #uuid "11f2b70f-bcb2-5554-a4f5-52982774bf66" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-898f435b-7b14-5b2f-ae7d-4bcad320ae8a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6e795028-7b52-57a5-b776-1234735687ba" "answer-ae08dcf3-62ce-5a52-845e-1dd4af782784" "answer-898f435b-7b14-5b2f-ae7d-4bcad320ae8a" "answer-5760a8e4-95a8-56ad-9ea9-5dd8de11365f" "answer-f0064606-8a67-5c4c-866a-5588c435a49b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6e795028-7b52-57a5-b776-1234735687ba" :answer/id #uuid "6e795028-7b52-57a5-b776-1234735687ba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=2u(t)-{v}^{2}(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+4u(t)+{v}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ae08dcf3-62ce-5a52-845e-1dd4af782784" :answer/id #uuid "ae08dcf3-62ce-5a52-845e-1dd4af782784" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=3u(t)-v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-898f435b-7b14-5b2f-ae7d-4bcad320ae8a" :answer/id #uuid "898f435b-7b14-5b2f-ae7d-4bcad320ae8a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=3u(t)-{v}^{2}(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+4u(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5760a8e4-95a8-56ad-9ea9-5dd8de11365f" :answer/id #uuid "5760a8e4-95a8-56ad-9ea9-5dd8de11365f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=3u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+10 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=2u(t)+v(t)-3\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f0064606-8a67-5c4c-866a-5588c435a49b" :answer/id #uuid "f0064606-8a67-5c4c-866a-5588c435a49b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=3{u}^{2}(t)-{v}^{2}(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=4u(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-13f56d3d-b69f-5f92-a641-c6c370a1ed21" :question/id #uuid "13f56d3d-b69f-5f92-a641-c6c370a1ed21" :question/math-academy-id "q-335383" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+5y(t)-12 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)-4x(t)+2y(t)+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. An equilibrium point of the system is $[2,\\,1]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting this equilibrium to the origin?" :question/worked-solution "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and $u=[\\begin{aligned}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⇒\\,x=u+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nIn coordinate form, this can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nx(t)=u(t)+2,\\,y(t)=v(t)+1,\\,{x}^{′}(t)={u}^{′}(t),\\,{v}^{′}(t)={y}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now substitute these into the given system.\n\n- Substituting into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =4x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+5y(t)-12 \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =4(u(t)+2)-(v(t)+1)^{2}+5(v(t)+1)-12 \\\\  & =4u(t)+8-({v}^{2}(t)+2v(t)+1)+5v(t)+5-12 \\\\  & =4u(t)+8-{v}^{2}(t)-2v(t)-1+5v(t)+5-12 \\\\  & =4u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+3v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{2}(t)-4x(t)+2y(t)+2 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) & =(u(t)+2)^{2}-4(u(t)+2)+2(v(t)+1)+2 \\\\  & =({u}^{2}(t)+4u(t)+4)-4u(t)-8+2v(t)+2+2 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)+4u(t)+4-4u(t)-8+2v(t)+4 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)+2v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=4u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+3v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+2v(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-15f03df0-1c1a-500e-83f5-21bfe8839deb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-15f03df0-1c1a-500e-83f5-21bfe8839deb" :answer-field/id #uuid "15f03df0-1c1a-500e-83f5-21bfe8839deb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-eb8d90ff-6933-5513-bc05-e3cc4c03b1d4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c461459c-bd05-5ec8-a414-0b65c4b944a7" "answer-192d2edf-e2b5-5feb-98f3-9b9f0bb71069" "answer-eb8d90ff-6933-5513-bc05-e3cc4c03b1d4" "answer-b901670b-07a9-55c2-a7e8-31763938cbe3" "answer-92c98d09-569a-504a-8066-e5395a7b1b79"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c461459c-bd05-5ec8-a414-0b65c4b944a7" :answer/id #uuid "c461459c-bd05-5ec8-a414-0b65c4b944a7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=2u(t)+2{v}^{2}(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+3v(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-192d2edf-e2b5-5feb-98f3-9b9f0bb71069" :answer/id #uuid "192d2edf-e2b5-5feb-98f3-9b9f0bb71069" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=2{u}^{2}(t)+2v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=4u(t)-3{v}^{2}(t)+3v(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eb8d90ff-6933-5513-bc05-e3cc4c03b1d4" :answer/id #uuid "eb8d90ff-6933-5513-bc05-e3cc4c03b1d4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=4u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+3v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+2v(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b901670b-07a9-55c2-a7e8-31763938cbe3" :answer/id #uuid "b901670b-07a9-55c2-a7e8-31763938cbe3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=u(t)+2{v}^{2}(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=4{u}^{2}(t)+3v(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-92c98d09-569a-504a-8066-e5395a7b1b79" :answer/id #uuid "92c98d09-569a-504a-8066-e5395a7b1b79" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+2{v}^{2}(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=4u(t)+3v(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186222500 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a system of ODEs such as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}' = \\mathbf{f}(x(t),y(t))\n$$\n\nwith a nonzero equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ satisfying\n$\\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{x}^\\ast) = \\mathbf{0}.$\n\nInstead of analyzing the system near a *nonzero* equilibrium $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast,$ we can introduce *shifted coordinates* so that the equilibrium becomes the origin.\nWe define the **deviation vector**\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{x}^\\ast,\n$$\n\nso that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{0} \\qquad\\Leftrightarrow\\qquad \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{x}^\\ast.\n$$\n\nThis transformed system has several advantages: shifting the equilibrium to the origin makes the system easier to analyze without altering its behavior.\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the following system of ODEs:\n\n![](images/47/4788b3521295a9a2ccdd0840940ff1c27263bb580ce72d787a06e1e1af50b9af.svg)\n\nThe equilibrium point of the system is $[1, \\, -1]^T.$ What is the system obtained by shifting the equilibrium to the origin?\n\nTo shift the system's equilibrium from $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}\n= \\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{x}^\\ast.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n![](images/04/04406c96333cbaabb72b49ad48aac2b144e2f21c01be688c1ad5f0cb6eda5391.svg)\n\nSubstituting $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf{x}'$ into the given system, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}^{′} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}](u+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}])+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is\n![](images/a7/a7d302836d0991995492abd0ccb35bb2701836fe4a4f50decfdd0866e7fecfae.svg)\n\nNotice that for linear systems, shifting the equilibrium to the origin just removes the constant term." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186222501 :tutorial/content "Recall that when we shift coordinates by defining\n\n$$\nu = x - x^\\ast,\\qquad v = y - y^\\ast,\n$$\n\nwe translate the coordinate system so that the equilibrium $(x^\\ast, y^\\ast)$ becomes $(0,0).$\n\nThis translation preserves several important properties of the system. Shifting coordinates does not change the dynamics of the system, it only changes where the equilibrium appears in the coordinate plane. In particular:\n\n1\\. *Eigenvalues do not change:*\n\n  After shifting a linear system, the eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors are unchanged.\n\n2\\. *Stability type is preserved:*\n\n  The equilibrium retains its original classification (saddle, node, spiral, center, etc.). Shifting does not alter the local behavior near the equilibrium.\n\n3\\. *Trajectory shapes are unchanged up to translation:*\n\n  Trajectories in the $(u,v)$-plane have the same shapes as those in the $(x,y)$-plane. Only their location changes.\n\n4\\. *Equilibria correspond one-to-one:*\n\n  The target equilibrium $(x^\\ast, y^\\ast)$ maps to $(u,v) = (0,0).$ The shift does not create or remove equilibria.\n\n5\\. *Nullclines translate but retain the same shape:*\n\n  The geometric structure of the vector field is preserved under translation.\n\nLet's proceed to some examples." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186221472 :activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
